<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gepids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gepidae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gepids were an East Germanic people closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, settled north of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> by the 3rd century. Subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, they led the revolt that won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454 and then ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> plain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, later taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>. Their kingdom was destroyed in 567 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, and the survivors were absorbed into the Avar Khaganate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gepids.jpg" alt="Gepids" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guriezous · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
